    "body": "Newfoundland and Labrador Prospectors Association President Norm Mercer (left) chats with Northern Miner host Devan Murugan.\nBy: Northern Miner Staff November 20, 2026\nNewfoundland and Labrador should put more money into prospector grants and mentoring as veteran prospectors retire, says Norm Mercer, president of the province’s prospectors association.\nFourteen years after its founding, the Newfoundland and Labrador Prospectors Association raised enough money this year to open an office and hire a part-time geologist, Mercer said early this month at Mineral Resources Review in St. John’s. The group wants to reach young people while they’re still in high school or just starting out in the trades.\n“It’s only going to be a small percentage who may go out and make a big discovery, but many could go out and find a mineral property that companies would come and do more work on and option those properties,” Mercer told The Northern Miner .\nThe first person to receive a provincial prospector’s grant was Al Chislett. In 1993, he and Chris Verbiski were looking for diamonds in Labrador when they spotted a streak of rusty rock from their helicopter. It led them to the nickel, copper and cobalt deposit at Voisey’s Bay.\nWatch the full video below:\nPrint\nTAG: Video\nREGION: Canada\nCOMPANY: Newfoundland and Labrador Prospectors Association\nRepublish this article",
